    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                An insurance company looks at its auto insurance customers and finds that
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            all insure at least one car
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            85% insure more than one car
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            23% insure a sports car
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            17% insure more than one car, including a sports car
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
<p>
        Find the probability that a customer selected at random insures exactly one car and it is not a sports car.
    </p>
    <p>
   <alert>Hint</alert>: Let the sample space be the set of all people who insure at least one car. Let <m>A</m> be the set of people insuring
    more than one car and <m>B</m> be the set of people insuring a sports car. Draw a Venn diagram.
</p>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
